    "episode_id": 45245,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/5/45245.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 1,
    "turn_text": "Hey, Steve, I'm doing so great today. I can't tell you how thrilled I am. Talk to our guests today. She's just really one of my favorite people, really, with somebody in the world who I admire so much. And to get to talk to her today is just both such an honor and such a joy. And so with that, I want to introduce Latipa Simon, She is a nationally recognized advocate for civil rights and racial justice in Oakland and the Bay Area. She is currently running to replace Representative Barbara Lee in Congress. In 2016, following the police murder of Oscar Grant, Latifah was elected to the Bay Area Rapid Transit Board of Directors, or as we call it here in the Bay, Bart. And she served as president of that board. And in 2005, she was tapped by then San Francisco DA Kamala Harris to spearhead back on track, which was the city's first youth anti recidivism program. At the tender age of 26, Lativa became the youngest woman ever to receive a MacArthur Genius fellowship. So we got a real certified genius in the house today in recognition of her work as executive director of the Young Women's Freedom Center. And in 2020, she was appointed a senior advisor on police reform for California Governor Gavin Newsom. Latifah has served on numerous boards and committees and has won lots and lots like Boku Awards including California State Assemblies, Woman of the Year in 2005. And I just want to say I can't wait to have my t-shirt. The district she's running for is the district I live in and I can't wait to have one that says Latifah in the house. So we are so glad to have a look. You can have that one for free. We got Latif on the house right here, and I'm just so stoked. And this is our third time on our podcast, Three Times a Charm. We love her so much, and Latif will welcome. So, so happy to have you here today.",
