    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/5/45245.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 12,
    "turn_text": "Well, just today. So I'm going to be very transparent. I had a wonderful meeting with a group called HLC. I'm forgetting what the acronym is, but it's a consortium of the biggest health care providers in this country. And I've been clear that I'm not taking resources from the executives of health care industry because of what I've seen. And there's another fight that I want to fight. So I told my campaign manager, can you make sure you email her before that I don't want their money or their endorsements? We got on the phone. And again, she was very clear. We're not supporting single payer or Medicare for all. But I was like, what are you supporting? I want to be an operator. I want to get shit done. I don't need to be on MSNBC every night. So she told me about their bill package. I said, OK, clearly we're not on some mountaintop unity stuff. But where can I work with you? Actually, they talked about a few immigration bills. They're trying to push through to make sure that folks who are pushing through their asylum status, who are LVMs, MAs, and even physicians actually have a place in this country because we have a shortage. We are going to disagree on so many things. But I just found so much comfort. And I am going. Bring in my 12-year-old. I'm a renter. I have no plans. I don't know where I'm going to live. I have four suits. But what I do know is that from what I've been through, I just buried Kevin's mother. Just a few months ago, I was her sole caretaker. And I went through all of her accordions and I found no life insurance. And we scraped to getting money to bury her pretty quickly, our church and our community. And just like in the last couple of weeks, she had to be in a rehab facility, which I learned was a nursing home that we deserve so much more. so much more. So that one call just actually really encouraged me to think like, I can work with institutions, but I don't have to agree with fully. But we actually have some common ground in it's my job to not bifurcate myself into a corner. I know who I am. I've been a radical pragmatist since my days running the Youngless Freedom Center. and I had to win things for my sisters. That's what I'm going to do in Congress, but with an elevated understanding of what's at stake. But the second meeting I had today, I'm not going to say no names, but it's a brother that has not been nice to me or not, and is a real serious sort of, I called him a provocateur. And we sat down and we had some gumbo at Brenda's, and I was like, you know, Tell me where we actually disagree. On values, there's one. Strategy is another because you can't tell me someone who is in the work, dishing on other people of color who are in politics. We all want to say for Oakland. I want to be able for Layla to play across the street at the park and I just watch her. We're not there yet, right? We all want folks to have stuff to eat. We all, if you want to If you sprain your ankle, you want to go get a cast. My job is to hold my politic, but also to bring in where possible, but also what is it? If you talk a lot online, what is it? We're now going to be meeting every other week. We're going to fight, and that's okay. When I ran the ambulance freedom center, I was in the tenderloin every single day, not just knocking on doors to give up referrals, but also confronting men who were exploiting the women that I was organizing with. And Barbara is amazing. She's telling me, sister, you know, you have to come in with your scarf and your suit. I'm like, okay, I got a lot to learn, but I'm a fighter. I'm a fighter. And I actually need those who I'm fighting against to be the closest to me. I mean, it's, it's a landscape organizing at its very best. So I owe that to my people, not just to hang around the choir. I don't, the choir is singing, the piano may be off tune, but I owe it to my folks to be able to confront folks who don't believe that we are human and that we deserve some of the most basic of rights. And not in a way that pushes them further. I do want to go to West Virginia to talk to mothers who can't get reproductive healthcare or the babies that they do have are going to school with bloody noses because they live in sacrifices outs. That's the work that I, I'm supposed to do at this point in my life to be able to bring and whip votes so that our folks can actually have a better life.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
