    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/5/45245.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 14,
    "turn_text": "You know, right after this call, I was speaking to a Jewish group who is somewhat upset at me because I have been pretty out about wanting to, will do, continue the legacy of Barbara Lee and Ron Delans, there's still wavering on that. There's also an acknowledgement on many fronts. But if you are an apnany Jew, you cannot live in Afghanistan. I'm a Muslim. It's Ramadan. I get it. I actually understand the geopolitics of that space. If you are In Iraqi Jew, you have no place to go, but the UK, the United States, where Israel. For many folks who are Jewish, not just the Askinaji population, they have no place to go. And, not but, and we have a responsibility as a core democratic republic state to ensure that our resources are going towards just regimes. I have babies. I have babies. So to me, there's 40,000 what we believe Hamas operatives in that region. And they have done horrible things. I understand. There are 30,000 people who are dead. And I know that violence doesn't be get violence. And finally, the United States government, the head of states are saying, This is out of hand. What we know is there are women on the ground and children and organizers of Jewish faith, of Islamic faith, of Christian faith, all over the world saying, we say this is not the way and you will come around. One of the things that Barbara told me is I told her in the beginning, oh my goodness, this is really intense at the very beginning of the conflict. She said, stand on the corner alone. People will come your way. if you are always standing on the right side of history. And peace, not looking through rose colored glasses, understanding, understanding what's happening in Ukraine, understanding what's happening in Haiti. But somebody needs to stand on the corner by themselves and say, this is not the way. You don't gain global dominance or peace through war. And it's not a position That is ill-informed. It is one that carries a tradition, Emily, in this seat, with some of the leaders that we know if they were still here, the ones that we celebrate on January 15th. Mr. King, Dr. King, and others who would purport the exact same lexicon. We can't do what we're doing. I'm very clear about that. I'm very clear about that. That's not going to make a lot of people happy. But at the end of the day, when I leave this life, I would be so, so thankful that I took the position that I took.",
